# LeakSanitizer suppressions for running the test suite under an
# ASan-instrumented extension inside an uninstrumented CPython.
#
# A pattern matches EVERY frame of a leak's allocation stack — including
# frames the symbolizer surfaces from inlining. Any allocation the
# extension makes from Python-called C has interpreter call machinery
# beneath it (_PyObject_VectorcallTstate inlined into method_vectorcall,
# _PyEval_EvalFrameDefault, ...), so even a prefix like leak:_PyObject_
# silently suppresses the extension's own leaks — observed with a planted
# leak, which only this allocator-level pattern set reports.
#
# Allocations CPython tracks bottom out in _PyMem_RawMalloc /
# _PyMem_RawCalloc / _PyMem_RawRealloc, and the shim's C never routes
# through them. The obmalloc entry points are listed too: on some paths
# (observed with realloc) the symbolizer does not surface the inlined
# _PyMem_Raw* frame, leaving _PyObject_Realloc as the deepest named
# allocator. All are allocator-only symbols — no call-machinery
# collision.
leak:_PyMem_Raw
leak:_PyObject_Malloc
leak:_PyObject_Calloc
leak:_PyObject_Realloc

# OpenSSL's one-time initialization registers its cleanup with atexit,
# which has not run when LSan scans. Only the init entry point is
# suppressed: a blanket CRYPTO_malloc would hide real leaks of SSL/X509
# objects.
leak:OPENSSL_init
